---
/*
  THE MIXED-KIND MOSAIC (2026-08-12, section 115).

  WHAT IT IS COPYING, and it is the one arrangement in the reference folders
  this site had never attempted. Rick Owens' editorial and process pages are not
  a grid of rows. They are a MOSAIC OF COLUMNS, and all 77 screenshots agree on
  its rules (section 107, `docs/SURVEY-RIFERIMENTI.md`):

    - block widths run from about 10% to 66% of the viewport and change every
      unit — 22%, 27%, 31%, 33%, 38%, 54%, 57%, 65% all appear;
    - the ANCHOR alternates: right, then left, then centre (`RO17`, `RO18`);
    - blocks are NOT top-aligned (`RO42`) and columns run past each other,
      ending where their own content ends (`RO34`, `RO38`);
    - the empty space is always to ONE SIDE, never centred, and the side
      alternates down the page (`RO19`, `RO37`);
    - adjacent blocks are of unlike KIND — prose beside a product beside a
      process photograph, in one row (`RO28`, `RO40`).

  WHY A FIXED CYCLE AND NOT A RANDOM ONE. Eight placements, repeated. The
  reference's asymmetry is composed, not scattered, and a random arrangement
  reads as a bug on the second visit. A cycle also means the page's rhythm is
  the same on every build, which is the only way a screenshot of it can be
  compared with a screenshot of it.

  THE PLACEMENTS ARE MEASURED OFF NAMED FRAMES, not chosen for balance:

    1  cols 1-5    left, medium          RO18 (lower cluster)
    2  cols 7-12   right, wide           RO18 (upper) / RO20 (the wide process
                                         frame beside a 27% column)
    3  cols 1-4    left cluster, first   RO14
    4  cols 5-8    left cluster, second  RO14 — the right third stays EMPTY,
                                         which is the whole point of that frame
    5  cols 3-10   centre, widest        RO17 (the acid-graded show set)
    6  cols 9-12   right, narrow         RO30 / RO31
    7  cols 1-3    left, narrowest       RO37
    8  cols 6-10   centre-right          RO41 / RO42

  Items 1+2, 3+4 and 7+8 can share a row because their columns do not overlap;
  5 and 6 do overlap and cannot. `grid-auto-flow: dense` places each block in
  the first row where its columns are free, which is what produces the
  reference's uneven, non-repeating rows out of a repeating cycle.

  THE THIRD KIND ARRIVED (2026-08-12, section 116). `RO40` and `RO28` — the two
  frames that make the whole device work — put a GARMENT CUT OUT ON PALE GROUND
  next to a full-bleed photograph next to a paragraph. Until tonight this mosaic
  ran on two of those three, and the note here said so.

  **There are now three cut-outs**, and they cover TWO pieces of eighteen:
  Monumentus Lux and Oblivion, the latter twice. So a mixed row is real where
  those two fall and is a two-kind row everywhere else, and that is the honest
  state of it — the arrangement is finished, the material is a fifth done.

  THREE MORE CAME BACK REDRAWN AND ARE NOT HERE. The tool that removed the
  backgrounds also straightened a raw hem, replaced a zip's teeth with a smooth
  bar, and rebuilt a matted fur as separate strands. On a brand whose claim is
  the raw cut, that is a lie about the object, so those three are not on the
  site in any form — not flagged, not provisional, absent. See
  `docs/SURVEY-MATERIALE-NUOVO.md` for which and how.

  A CUT-OUT IS GIVEN ROOM, NOT A MATCHING BOX (`RO40`). It sits small inside a
  narrow column with white around it, while the photograph beside it fills its
  box edge to edge. The photograph is a rectangle and the cutout is a shape;
  the white is what they share, and matching their heights would destroy it.

  ON A PHONE THERE IS NO MOSAIC. One column, full bleed, in order. Half of
  390px is 195px, and a 25% column of it is 97px — the reference is a desktop
  composition and both folders are honest about that (`VW27` stacks, `RO`'s
  clusters stack).
*/
import MediaSurface from "./MediaSurface.astro";
import Spine from "./Spine.astro";
import type {MediaItem} from "./../lib/media";
import type {Locale} from "../lib/locales";

/**
 * A block in the run. Three kinds, which is what the reference rows are made
 * of: prose, a garment cut out on pale ground, and a photograph.
 */
export type MosaicBlock =
  | {kind: "frame"; media: MediaItem}
  | {
      /** A garment cut out on the page's own paper. Given room, never a box. */
      kind: "product";
      media: MediaItem;
      /** The piece's name, and where it goes. Rendered as `RO36` does it. */
      label?: string | null;
      href?: string | null;
    }
  | {
      kind: "note";
      text: string;
      /** Set only where the text is ours and still unapproved. */
      marked?: boolean;
      /** The draft/translation notice, already localised by the caller. */
      notice?: string | null;
    };

interface Props {
  blocks: MosaicBlock[];
  locale: Locale;
  class?: string;
}

const {blocks, locale, class: className = ""} = Astro.props;

/*
  THE CYCLE. `start` is the 1-based grid column; `span` is how many of twelve.
  `drop` offsets a block downward by its own row's rhythm, which is `RO42`: two
  blocks in one row, deliberately not top-aligned. Two of eight carry it, so it
  reads as composition rather than as jitter.
*/
const PLACEMENTS = [
  {start: 1, span: 5, drop: false},
  {start: 7, span: 6, drop: true},
  {start: 1, span: 4, drop: false},
  {start: 5, span: 4, drop: false},
  {start: 3, span: 8, drop: false},
  {start: 9, span: 4, drop: false},
  {start: 1, span: 3, drop: false},
  {start: 6, span: 5, drop: true},
];

/*
  `sizes` HAS TO TELL THE TRUTH, and in this component it changes per block
  (section 69: a `sizes` that lies is the trap this project has already paid
  for twice). A five-column block at desktop is 5/12 of the viewport; on a
  phone every block is the full width. The number below is computed from the
  span rather than written out, so a placement edited above cannot leave a
  stale declaration behind it.
*/
const sizesFor = (span: number) =>
  `(min-width: 64rem) ${Math.round((span / 12) * 100)}vw, 100vw`;
---

<div class:list={["mosaic", className]}>
  {
    blocks.map((block, index) => {
      const place = PLACEMENTS[index % PLACEMENTS.length];
      const style = `--m-start:${place.start}; --m-span:${place.span}`;
      return (
        <div class:list={["mosaic-cell", place.drop && "mosaic-drop"]} style={style}>
          {block.kind === "frame" ? (
            <MediaSurface
              media={block.media}
              locale={locale}
              height="native"
              sizes={sizesFor(place.span)}
            />
          ) : block.kind === "product" ? (
            /*
              THE CUT-OUT, GIVEN ROOM (`RO40`, `RO36`).

              It is inset inside its own column rather than filling it, so the
              white around the shape is part of the composition — which is the
              difference between a cut-out and a sticker. Rick Owens puts a belt
              alone in a centre column at about a fifth of the space allotted to
              it; the inset here is the site's own rhythm token rather than a
              new number.

              THE NAME IS A LINE OF TEXT WITH A RULE UNDER IT, not a button
              (`RO36`: `CLICK TO BUY SUICIDEBAND COLLECTION`, underlined,
              centred, no border and no fill). That is how their editorial
              sells, and it is the same `hairline-under` every action on this
              site already uses.
            */
            <div class="mosaic-product">
              <MediaSurface
                media={block.media}
                locale={locale}
                height="native"
                sizes={sizesFor(place.span)}
              />
              {block.label &&
                (block.href ? (
                  <a href={block.href} class="type-label text-label hairline-under self-start">
                    {block.label}
                  </a>
                ) : (
                  <span class="type-label text-label">{block.label}</span>
                ))}
            </div>
          ) : (
            /*
              A NOTE IS A TEXT SURFACE AND TAKES THE SPINE, which is the site's
              rule for every surface that carries words (section 2). Inside the
              mosaic the spine is the block's own left edge rather than the
              page's margin, because that edge is what the column actually has.
            */
            <div class="mosaic-note relative">
              <Spine />
              {block.marked && block.notice && (
                <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{block.notice}</p>
              )}
              <p
                class:list={[
                  "type-body text-body whitespace-pre-line",
                  block.marked && "draft-text",
                ]}
              >
                {block.text}
              </p>
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      );
    })
  }
</div>
